#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192256 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc1fbb99-83e1-52ba-83f1-367de07351a6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Improper Integral Using a Substitution"}] :question/id #uuid "522a785c-3e1e-5515-82c9-8980b448bee4" :question/math-academy-id "e-10553" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_1^\\infty  x^3\\cdot 2^{-x^4}   \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=x^4.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= 4x^3\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\dfrac 14 \\textrm{d}u =x^3\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty$ when $x\\to \\infty,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x^4$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{3}\\cdot {2}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{2}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{3}\\cdot {2}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{2}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{2}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{2}^{-u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{2}^{-u}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{2}^{a}}-\\frac{1}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\dfrac{1}{2^x},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} \\dfrac{1}{2^{x}} = \\infty$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} \\dfrac{1}{2^{x}} = 0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/assets/1969145d74662468ec23d458ac1ad0194d51cbf6b026718b7232466e9357efdf.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{3}\\cdot {2}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{2}^{a}}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot (0-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192257 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f0d268f-97f9-5db8-863d-dfc237afa546" :knowledge-point/title "Improper Integral Involving the Exponential Function"}] :question/id #uuid "6ad02a19-7b07-5e07-8388-b46fd1cc48ae" :question/math-academy-id "e-10554" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^\\infty   e^{-3x}  \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int e^{px + q}\\,\\textrm d x = \\dfrac1p e^{px+q} + C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{-1}^{a}{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{3}\\,]}_{-1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{e}^{-3x}]}_{-1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-3a}-{e}^{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=e^{-3x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} e^{-3x} = \\infty$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} e^{-3x} =0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/assets/bc4ffc41105491f2bd6b37b2482afc64946d8f67f6db83dfa322c400ae4bc2b6.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }{e}^{-3x} & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-3a}-{e}^{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (0-{e}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192292 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e84d5ab0-cec4-5988-9db3-8e296590d279" :knowledge-point/title "Improper Integral Involving Other Exponential Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "37af544e-feab-52b0-bcef-887c723093b0" :question/math-academy-id "e-10764" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{0}  3\\cdot 2^{2x+1}   \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int b^{\\,px + q}\\,\\textrm d x = \\dfrac1{p\\ln(b)} b^{\\,px+q} + C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to-\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3\\cdot {2}^{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{2}^{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{2}^{2x+1}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot {2}^{1}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{2}^{2x}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({2}^{0}-{2}^{2a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{2a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=2^{2x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} 2^{2x} = 0$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} 2^{2x} = \\infty$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/assets/009f467677932d0ac17e1632c31f4d3aa5271ffe332730d9670e69862b243bef.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3\\cdot {2}^{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{2a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301297 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e84d5ab0-cec4-5988-9db3-8e296590d279" :knowledge-point/title "Improper Integral Involving Other Exponential Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fb9c396-827e-5b37-a80b-769aab9304e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301299} {:answer/id #uuid "2aef1160-a55c-54c8-899d-bfe73083d87a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301300} {:answer/id #uuid "d5b6165f-13d7-5d98-8ea6-dc1e93025e15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301301} {:answer/id #uuid "99f3b189-b8e8-5876-8f51-1910936e6078" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301302} {:answer/id #uuid "d7bf6940-d273-566f-ae3c-97863e5f089b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301303}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99f3b189-b8e8-5876-8f51-1910936e6078" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301302} :answer-field/id #uuid "02a401f7-41c4-5faa-99ac-1eac8afda80a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301298}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3c351560-bc86-5c54-b119-540a5f2a9855" :question/math-academy-id "q-152230" :question/problem "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{2}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{2}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{1}{2}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{2}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{2}^{x}]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({2}^{1}-{2}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (2-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={2}^{x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{2}^{x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{2}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/assets/b9a29cbd8a029e0f26e3da6a042ada0f8a809053233979bb4ff73db3dcf0f10d.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{2}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (2-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301304 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc1fbb99-83e1-52ba-83f1-367de07351a6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Improper Integral Using a Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c6c5f70-00d1-5a54-b01e-db7971bac1e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301306} {:answer/id #uuid "b434f781-bdfd-5c1e-9ae1-6d1576222f04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301307} {:answer/id #uuid "f9d69669-7e91-5a13-846a-a2f8cbc90b45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301308} {:answer/id #uuid "90b38331-243d-51df-8164-257da4fc09c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301309} {:answer/id #uuid "074fbbb0-a683-5125-bd12-3376ff86de95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301310}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9d69669-7e91-5a13-846a-a2f8cbc90b45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301308} :answer-field/id #uuid "75443f7c-fb29-5ae5-8338-a69542945ea3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301305}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8038a520-14dc-557f-9ace-9c284278dbad" :question/math-academy-id "q-152417" :question/problem "${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{2}^{1-{x}^{2}}x\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=1-{x}^{2}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2x\\,⟹\\,-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=x\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=1-{x}^{2}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $-\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{2}^{1-{x}^{2}}x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{-\\infty }{2}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{2}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{2}^{1-{x}^{2}}x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{2}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{2}^{u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[{2}^{u}]_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(1-{2}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y={2}^{a},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{a}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{2}^{a}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/assets/15f110a3a228573a5ec349664615293be38fff8c4bd4f14a8ec1b11b997cb818.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{2}^{1-{x}^{2}}x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot (1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301311 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e84d5ab0-cec4-5988-9db3-8e296590d279" :knowledge-point/title "Improper Integral Involving Other Exponential Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dcd669c5-15c8-58a2-8b64-7ef90c4a3660" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301313} {:answer/id #uuid "0f4915c4-b96b-5786-9e1d-7b9cba4d0491" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301314} {:answer/id #uuid "ba37b007-40c8-5909-9627-1e2981b3f99e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301315} {:answer/id #uuid "10c5eeb2-e0fc-5da5-95da-ed99d9cc86e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301316} {:answer/id #uuid "2d9b5cac-d412-5e11-a273-42a30a5cebd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301317}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f4915c4-b96b-5786-9e1d-7b9cba4d0491" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301314} :answer-field/id #uuid "aaae668d-9b89-5711-9627-f309743a340a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301312}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "969bf2aa-8cb3-5087-a3e3-258b63062534" :question/math-academy-id "q-152875" :question/problem "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}2\\cdot {5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}2\\cdot {5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{1}{5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{5}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{5}^{x}]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({5}^{1}-{5}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (5-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{5}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={5}^{x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{5}^{x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{5}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/assets/06c51ecc423683f4377248004b7a318f1348836bd752a7f65f94457d1774e64d.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}2\\cdot {5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (5-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{5}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (5-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301318 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e84d5ab0-cec4-5988-9db3-8e296590d279" :knowledge-point/title "Improper Integral Involving Other Exponential Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51d484ac-9937-59cc-b38b-8d36b365f700" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301320} {:answer/id #uuid "80d868ec-c656-54c7-80ec-4aaf0b9b7b74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301321} {:answer/id #uuid "03073303-9350-5466-b303-203ed560db91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301322} {:answer/id #uuid "f01235d5-adfa-5808-ad11-8aac14a930d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{24}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301323} {:answer/id #uuid "a320fa96-3dd2-51cd-9a41-771e5ec11a52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301324}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f01235d5-adfa-5808-ad11-8aac14a930d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{24}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301323} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb315466-dfca-501c-ac07-0ff5e99cad60" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301319}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "393d72cd-a037-522f-8ffe-6b40d5fb5ab2" :question/math-academy-id "q-152877" :question/problem "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{3}3\\cdot {2}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{3}3\\cdot {2}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{3}{2}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{2}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}]}_{a}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{2}^{x}]}_{a}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({2}^{3}-{2}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (8-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={2}^{x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{2}^{x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{2}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/assets/b9a29cbd8a029e0f26e3da6a042ada0f8a809053233979bb4ff73db3dcf0f10d.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{3}3\\cdot {2}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (8-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (8-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301325 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f0d268f-97f9-5db8-863d-dfc237afa546" :knowledge-point/title "Improper Integral Involving the Exponential Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35c33afe-4c79-570a-ad4b-1aba78b00479" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301327} {:answer/id #uuid "9e3b06fa-cd87-591a-b95f-259395ba7412" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301328} {:answer/id #uuid "6fc20493-7c3d-5625-8de1-dffe627ee2cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301329} {:answer/id #uuid "343f0df1-4ecc-5075-82fb-85e64eae0b77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301330} {:answer/id #uuid "81869388-99e1-5279-9e9f-d09619be9037" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301331}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "343f0df1-4ecc-5075-82fb-85e64eae0b77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301330} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5ab81b5-3047-554d-a157-6cbf4f7f74b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301326}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5fcd2373-835d-58c0-a150-1972239e0d50" :question/math-academy-id "q-152884" :question/problem "${\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }{e}^{-2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int {e}^{px+q}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{p}{e}^{px+q}+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }{e}^{-2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{-1}^{a}{e}^{-2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-2x-1}]}_{-1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-1}}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{e}^{-2x}]}_{-1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2e}({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{2a}}-{e}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{e}^{2x}},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{2x}}=\\infty $\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{2x}}=0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/assets/0064a1c234adc9cf5d28d16696bef8a72384a8cc9f85ecc865c5d4ba36dc0d49.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }{e}^{-2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{2e}({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{2a}}-{e}^{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2e}(0-{e}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301332 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e84d5ab0-cec4-5988-9db3-8e296590d279" :knowledge-point/title "Improper Integral Involving Other Exponential Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c5dcbf9-e43b-5d89-bc98-77878fbe3b29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{e}^{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301334} {:answer/id #uuid "c2bf7242-1dfb-548a-bc3a-a20d63749525" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301335} {:answer/id #uuid "2f731fd3-82ae-55a9-8245-731d881d6b3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301336} {:answer/id #uuid "5af18c35-79f7-5e01-b585-b7964212c024" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301337} {:answer/id #uuid "35ef47f4-95f4-53a5-9912-c4bf11ac6a31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301338}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35ef47f4-95f4-53a5-9912-c4bf11ac6a31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301338} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e0ff1bb-d49d-5f2a-b53d-b9a7ac2b1026" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301333}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "56801e6d-5cbb-50ba-b004-b5f220ae4a1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-20501" :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{5}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int {b}^{\\,px+q}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{p\\operatorname{ln}⁡(b)}{b}^{\\,px+q}+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{5}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{5}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{-{5}^{-x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{5}^{-x}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{5}^{a}}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{5}^{x}},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{5}^{x}}=\\infty $\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{5}^{x}}=0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/assets/5949cbdface45cf103ae5bbd14db5d81917c19f3ef82417216e26d2064ca6bd1.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{5}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{5}^{a}}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193070 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc1fbb99-83e1-52ba-83f1-367de07351a6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Improper Integral Using a Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b04158bd-8b5c-53e3-b00e-0e5e20eda3a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191192} {:answer/id #uuid "bd4e7957-ae3a-58e4-a82a-6d62e142f275" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191193} {:answer/id #uuid "04ba4369-c485-555e-ae2c-de879c990dc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191194} {:answer/id #uuid "211cfcf9-6419-5a27-ba51-5cedc1f607ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191195} {:answer/id #uuid "70316794-503a-5bfc-ac8a-8f094446e8d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191196}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd4e7957-ae3a-58e4-a82a-6d62e142f275" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191193} :answer-field/id #uuid "3143fafd-7355-519f-8915-bb5002dd4cd4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191191}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "826720bb-562f-595c-b16e-c49b0073360a" :question/math-academy-id "q-20891" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{2x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=\\sqrt{x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,⟹\\,2\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\sqrt{x}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{2x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-\\sqrt{2}u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{2x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-\\sqrt{2}u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-\\sqrt{2}u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{2}u}}{\\sqrt{2}}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{e}^{-\\sqrt{2}u}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\sqrt{2}a}}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\sqrt{2}x}},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\sqrt{2}x}}=\\infty $\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\sqrt{2}x}}=0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/assets/5e9932629e5a201c2ada61157fdfa39351962a55116e2980157b1823907159f2.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{2x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\sqrt{2}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\sqrt{2}a}}-1) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301339 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f0d268f-97f9-5db8-863d-dfc237afa546" :knowledge-point/title "Improper Integral Involving the Exponential Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a71285fa-f291-5636-98d6-005c3db37412" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5e^{-9}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301341} {:answer/id #uuid "3aeb9ff1-6aef-5f6b-b8e8-0191d058f821" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5e^{-9}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301342}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3aeb9ff1-6aef-5f6b-b8e8-0191d058f821" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5e^{-9}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301342} :answer-field/id #uuid "2899872b-928a-5cd9-be75-01d45bdf6c7f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301340}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "89d283ba-d227-577d-b377-aac9acea03b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-350810" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-3}\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int {e}^{\\,px+q}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{p}{e}^{\\,px+q}+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-3}\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-3}{e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3x}]}_{a}^{-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-9}-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3{e}^{9}}-\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{3a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{3x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{3x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{3x}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/assets/2f4fb9b35e0b68c6155ab9b945d10c36d08d878560e93d34d04e610d1983e50c.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-3}\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3{e}^{9}}-\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{3a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}(\\frac{1}{3{e}^{9}}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6{e}^{9}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301343 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f0d268f-97f9-5db8-863d-dfc237afa546" :knowledge-point/title "Improper Integral Involving the Exponential Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06fe1340-759b-586c-837e-bd624a59f9d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5e^4}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301345}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06fe1340-759b-586c-837e-bd624a59f9d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5e^4}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301345} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4f2aa46-8db9-554e-a213-55c6023dfb62" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301344}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c0191a30-c2e2-52a8-94ee-6284596521e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-350830" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }10{e}^{-4x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int {e}^{px+q}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{p}{e}^{px+q}+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }10{e}^{-4x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{-1}^{a}10{e}^{-4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{-1}^{a}{e}^{-4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-4x}]}_{-1}^{a} \\\\  & =10\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-4a}+\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{4}) \\\\  & =10\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{4}-\\frac{1}{4}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-4a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{-4x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{-4x}=\\infty $\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{-4x}=0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/assets/ade58ce56684420fb0f00236af50bbb54f76801fc8870e1cce135cbd51f1b726.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }10{e}^{-4x}\\,\\text{d}x & =10\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{4}-\\frac{1}{4}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-4a}) \\\\  & =10(\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{5{e}^{4}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301346 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f0d268f-97f9-5db8-863d-dfc237afa546" :knowledge-point/title "Improper Integral Involving the Exponential Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31fdc5d2-1d35-5fea-9c4b-918153ee0c9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e^2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301348} {:answer/id #uuid "82fbe0aa-009e-538e-b428-1990e4b27f10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e^2}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301349}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31fdc5d2-1d35-5fea-9c4b-918153ee0c9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e^2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301348} :answer-field/id #uuid "254750a4-a236-52f8-94b7-2ada10d7b8d2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301347}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7abe7254-179c-5b02-b7ca-239c617b5af3" :question/math-academy-id "q-350845" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-3x+2}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int {e}^{px+q}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{p}{e}^{px+q}+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-3x+2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-3x+2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3x+2}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3a+2}+\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{{e}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{e}^{2}}{3}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-3a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{-3x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{-3x}=\\infty $\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{-3x}=0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/assets/425e55256d2e53db1fce6684e2b7b05e8b487d5107dd4abacb8cf17add287c23.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-3x+2}\\,\\text{d}x & =(\\frac{{e}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{e}^{2}}{3}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-3a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{e}^{2}}{3}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301350 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f0d268f-97f9-5db8-863d-dfc237afa546" :knowledge-point/title "Improper Integral Involving the Exponential Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ddc04d1-887b-5b34-8b0a-412f0e41741c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e^{-7}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301352}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ddc04d1-887b-5b34-8b0a-412f0e41741c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e^{-7}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301352} :answer-field/id #uuid "917aa21b-ff2f-51d7-abda-effc9871c024" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301351}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "854f43d5-dc5a-5602-b401-4fa8802d3c1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-350847" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{e}^{-4x-3}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int {e}^{px+q}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{p}{e}^{px+q}+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{e}^{-4x-3}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{e}^{-4x-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-4x-3}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-4a-3}+\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-7}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{4{e}^{7}}-\\frac{1}{4{e}^{3}}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-4a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{-4x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{-4x}=\\infty $\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{-4x}=0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/assets/dd3903454e30d878fee3cd1f58f3942db034fde534e514f99e2c14ddd892a9b3.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{e}^{-4x-3}\\,\\text{d}x & =(\\frac{1}{4{e}^{7}}-\\frac{1}{4{e}^{3}}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-4a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4{e}^{7}}-\\frac{1}{4{e}^{3}}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4{e}^{7}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301353 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e84d5ab0-cec4-5988-9db3-8e296590d279" :knowledge-point/title "Improper Integral Involving Other Exponential Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba89a20e-ecec-5f2f-974d-efb594302db0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{\\ln(5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301355}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba89a20e-ecec-5f2f-974d-efb594302db0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{\\ln(5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301355} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a136a6e-5687-5056-8d3d-14542c6260d6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301354}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ee1a35ee-9fb2-528c-9b40-05d78a32e70f" :question/math-academy-id "q-351417" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{5}^{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int {b}^{\\,px+q}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{p\\operatorname{ln}⁡(b)}{b}^{\\,px+q}+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{5}^{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{5}^{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{5\\cdot {5}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{5}^{x}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(1-{5}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{5}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={5}^{x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{5}^{x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{5}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/assets/2fc15149d2097a1965e575e725b4dfb49401c1fe5bb1c5b3e43bf0f4f51743a1.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{5}^{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{5}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301356 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc1fbb99-83e1-52ba-83f1-367de07351a6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Improper Integral Using a Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d2b1a32-5ab9-588d-80de-b21ffe231aff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301358}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d2b1a32-5ab9-588d-80de-b21ffe231aff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301358} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c2c721b-d336-528e-8596-b9b78c5776b2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301357}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b7dd0fc4-1e7b-59a0-83e5-4685362a10c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-351513" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{3x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=\\sqrt{x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,⟹\\,2\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\sqrt{x}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{3x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-\\sqrt{3}u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{3x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-\\sqrt{3}u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-\\sqrt{3}u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{3}u}}{\\sqrt{3}}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{e}^{-\\sqrt{3}u}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\sqrt{3}a}}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\sqrt{3}x}},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}}=\\infty $\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}}=0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/assets/1c0fc31d392bbaccf9a292553321bd60ccbdb2a02f3675543797f570c4fa0ef1.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{3x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\sqrt{3}a}}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301359 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc1fbb99-83e1-52ba-83f1-367de07351a6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Improper Integral Using a Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37cacb50-acde-5f4c-98ec-48ef18e314f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\ln(4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301361} {:answer/id #uuid "81bf3d4b-df09-53f0-9589-1ccd022fa30e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301362}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37cacb50-acde-5f4c-98ec-48ef18e314f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\ln(4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301361} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad63441b-0d19-55c1-8d5b-d23447518684" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301360}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "680b7856-af9d-5109-9fd9-00152a4dd785" :question/math-academy-id "q-351523" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\cdot {4}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\cdot {4}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{4}^{u}}\\,\\text{d}u={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{4}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\cdot {4}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{4}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{4}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{4}^{-u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{4}^{-u}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{4}^{a}}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{4}^{x}},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{4}^{x}}=\\infty $\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{4}^{x}}=0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/assets/e50ca6e9326f5a6146a7a486adcc2d3d5f5c059eae3e2e1b06fbff8664b1c7df.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\cdot {4}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{4}^{a}}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301363 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc1fbb99-83e1-52ba-83f1-367de07351a6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Improper Integral Using a Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04a0d85a-fe9a-507f-b5f8-9c432bbc2d27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301365}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04a0d85a-fe9a-507f-b5f8-9c432bbc2d27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301365} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab529495-7f7e-562d-8045-f19c007d8fff" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301364}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5e754189-9978-5a00-8148-3768cbdf2063" :question/math-academy-id "q-351539" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{3/x-1}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=\\frac{1}{x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,⟹\\,-\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to {0}^{-}$ and $u\\to 0$ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\frac{1}{x}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-\\infty $ | $0$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{3/x-1}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{0}^{-\\infty }{e}^{3u-1}\\,\\text{d}u={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{e}^{3u-1}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{3/x-1}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{e}^{3u-1}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{e}^{3u-1}}{3}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{-1}-{e}^{3a-1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-1}}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{3a-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{3x-1}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{3x-1}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{3x-1}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/assets/6a5fd63a29d7c8f5fba712c7fa6c20620c9a1438750f3197606e726a85666827.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{3/x-1}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{e}^{-1}}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{3a-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-1}}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
